Looking at this mutation: `$120 = v7>=-3` (since `3'h5` as signed 3-bit is -3) and `$121 = v7<=3` (always true for 3-bit values). The original `$122 = $120 & $121` computes "v7 != -4" — the in-box check for coordinate v7. Bit-0 inversion of `$121` (a 1-bit wire) turns it into `NOT(v7<=3)`, which is always false, so `$122` collapses to always-0. This makes the in_box signal permanently false, forcing every valid input pair to be REFUSED regardless of actual box membership — a massive behavioral change, not equivalent.

Testing with the all-zero vectors: originally p=0 so certified fires (trivial branch); under the mutant, in_box is stuck low, so refused fires instead.

